<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <PackageId>DeepSharp.Import.PyTorch</PackageId>
    <Description>Reads a network PyTorch trained into the same network written with DeepSharp: new SafetensorsFile(network, loss).Read(stream) for a safetensors file, new TorchSaveFile(network, loss).Read(stream) for the .pt or .pth file torch.save(model.state_dict(), file) writes. Every number goes into the slot its name names — a linear layer's weights turned round, a convolution's kernel laid out channels last, the rows a flatten makes of images read row by row where PyTorch reads them channel by channel — and the network and its loss come back, to serve, to measure or to train further. A safetensors file is read by a port of safetensors' own reader, so a file it refuses is refused here in its words. The pickle torch.save writes is a program, so it is read by an interpreter that carries out only what PyTorch's own weights-only reader carries out and builds nothing a file names but what a state dictionary is made of: any other name is refused where the file names it, before anything is looked up, built or run. Every fault among the tensors is named at once by the tensor's name, and a network takes all of a file's numbers or none of them.</Description>
    <PackageTags>deepsharp;pytorch;safetensors;torch-save;pt;pth;pickle;import;weights;state-dict;neural-network;deep-learning;dotnet</PackageTags>
    <!-- Nothing here may look a type up by a name a file holds, so nothing here may need reflection an application's trimming
         would take away: the analyser says so at build time, for every framework this package is built for. -->
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Onnxify.Safetensors 0.3.11, MIT (github.com/gluschenko/onnxify, commit 07a511e): a managed port of safetensors' own
         reader, tensor.rs, for .NET 8 and .NET 10, with nothing of its own beside it. Borrowed rather than written: it
         refuses what the reference refuses. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Onnxify.Safetensors" Version="0.3.11" />
    <!-- No pickle reader is borrowed for torch.save's files, and why is measured. Razorvine.Pickle 1.5.0 (MIT, 58,368 bytes,
         .NET Standard 2.0) reads PyTorch's pickle only once constructors are registered in a dictionary every unpickler in
         the process shares, and builds something for every name a file holds — a dictionary for os.system, System.Decimal
         for decimal.Decimal — so it cannot refuse a name before building for it. TorchSharp.PyBridge brings TorchSharp and
         Razorvine beneath it; PyTorchCheckpoint 0.1.0 is built for .NET 10 alone and keeps any name it meets. PyTorch itself
         reads weights through an interpreter of its own, torch/_weights_only_unpickler.py, and so does this package. -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DeepSharp\DeepSharp.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\assets\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
